  "assumptions": [
    "Idealized model: small-swing period T = 2π√(L/g) with g = 9.81 m/s², so 1 m gives about 2.01 s and 4 m would double it.",
    "The string is light and doesn't stretch, and the bob counts as a single point. There is no air resistance or friction, so the swing never dies down.",
    "The swing starts at a fixed 10° angle. At that angle the real period is only about 0.2% longer than the small-swing formula gives.",
    "The swing angle is computed from the timeline time, so changing the length (or the 60 s loop wrapping round) can make the bob jump to a new point in its swing."
  ],
  "program": {
    "source": "const G=9.81,A=10*Math.PI/180;\nexport function evaluate({inputs,time}){const L=inputs.length;const T=2*Math.PI*Math.sqrt(L/G);const th=A*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*time/T);const h=th/2;return{nodes:{arm:{quaternion:[0,0,Math.sin(h),Math.cos(h)]},string:{position:[0,-L/2,0],scale:[1,L,1]},bob:{position:[0,-L,0]}},readouts:{period:T,len:L}};}"
  }
